  <section id="average" aria-labelledby="average-h">
    <h2 id="average-h">Average Wrist Size for Men and Women</h2>
    <p>The average wrist is <strong>6.9&nbsp;inches (17.6&nbsp;cm) for men</strong> and <strong>6.1&nbsp;inches (15.5&nbsp;cm) for women</strong>, measured at the wrist bone in the U.S. Army ANSUR&nbsp;II survey of more than 6,000 adults. Men&rsquo;s wrists are on average about 0.8&nbsp;inch (2&nbsp;cm) larger.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/average-wrist-size-chart.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Average wrist size chart: distribution of wrist circumference for men and women and median wrist size by height"><figcaption>How wrist sizes spread for men and women, and how the median rises with height.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Wrist size percentiles</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">5th</th><th scope="col">25th</th><th scope="col">50th</th><th scope="col">75th</th><th scope="col">95th</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>6.4 in (16.2)</td><td>6.7 in (17.0)</td><td>6.9 in (17.6)</td><td>7.2 in (18.2)</td><td>7.5 in (19.0)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>5.6 in (14.2)</td><td>5.9 in (15.0)</td><td>6.1 in (15.5)</td><td>6.3 in (16.0)</td><td>6.6 in (16.8)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Wrist size varies far less than other body measurements: 90% of men fall within about 1.1&nbsp;inches of each other. That is because the wrist is mostly bone, tendon and skin, with very little fat or muscle.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ranks" aria-labelledby="ranks-h">
    <h2 id="ranks-h">Is Your Wrist Small, Average or Large?</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Where common wrist sizes rank (percentile)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Wrist</th><th scope="col">Men</th><th scope="col">Women</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">5.5 in (14.0 cm)</th><td>1st</td><td>3rd</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6.0 in (15.2 cm)</th><td>1st</td><td>37th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6.5 in (16.5 cm)</th><td>11th</td><td>90th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7.0 in (17.8 cm)</th><td>58th</td><td>99th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">7.5 in (19.1 cm)</th><td>95th</td><td>99th</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">8.0 in (20.3 cm)</th><td>99th</td><td>99th</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>A 7&nbsp;inch wrist is just above average for a man; 7.5&nbsp;inches is in the top 5%, and fewer than 1 in 100 men measure 8&nbsp;inches or more.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="by-height" aria-labelledby="by-height-h">
    <h2 id="by-height-h">Average Wrist Size by Height</h2>
    <p>Taller people have larger wrists: the median rises by roughly a tenth of an inch for every inch of height. Use these medians to judge your wrist against people your own size.</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Median wrist circumference by height (ANSUR II)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Height</th><th scope="col">Men</th><th scope="col">Women</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 2&ndash;3 in</th><td>6.5 in</td><td>6.0 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 4&ndash;5 in</th><td>6.7 in</td><td>6.1 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 6&ndash;7 in</th><td>6.8 in</td><td>6.3 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 8&ndash;9 in</th><td>6.9 in</td><td>6.4 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5 ft 10&ndash;11 in</th><td>7.0 in</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6 ft 0&ndash;1 in</th><td>7.1 in</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">6 ft 2&ndash;3 in</th><td>7.3 in</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="frame" aria-labelledby="frame-h">
    <h2 id="frame-h">What Your Wrist Says About Frame Size</h2>
    <p>Because the wrist carries so little fat, doctors and dietitians use it to estimate skeletal frame size. The most common method divides height by wrist circumference:</p>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Frame size from height &divide; wrist circumference</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Small frame</th><th scope="col">Medium frame</th><th scope="col">Large frame</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Men</th><td>above 10.4</td><td>9.6&ndash;10.4</td><td>below 9.6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Women</th><td>above 11.0</td><td>10.1&ndash;11.0</td><td>below 10.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Frame size helps explain why two people of the same height can have very different healthy weights. Get a full result, with BMI context, from the <a href="/body-frame-size-calculator/">body frame size calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-measure" aria-labelledby="how-to-measure-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-measure-h">How to Measure Your Wrist</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Find the wrist bone.</strong> Rest your forearm on a table, palm up. Find the small bony bump on the little-finger side of the wrist.</li><li><strong>Wrap the tape.</strong> Wrap a soft tape measure around the wrist just above that bone, on the hand side, where a watch would sit. Keep the wrist straight and relaxed.</li><li><strong>Keep it snug.</strong> The tape should lie flat on the skin without pressing in. If you use a strip of paper or string, mark where it overlaps and measure it against a ruler.</li><li><strong>Read and record.</strong> Read the measurement to the nearest eighth of an inch or 0.5 cm. Measure both wrists; a difference of a few millimeters is normal.</li></ol>
    <p>No tape? Wrap a strip of paper or a phone charger cable around the wrist, mark it and lay it against a ruler. Our <a href="/body-measurements/">body measurements guide</a> covers the other tape positions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="watch" aria-labelledby="watch-h">
    <h2 id="watch-h">Wrist Size for Watches and Bracelets</h2>
    <p>For watches, the case should sit within the width of the wrist, with the lugs not hanging over the edge. A rough guide by wrist circumference:</p>
    <ul class="tp-steps" style="list-style:disc">
      <li><strong>Under 6&nbsp;in:</strong> 28&ndash;36&nbsp;mm cases.</li>
      <li><strong>6&ndash;6.5&nbsp;in:</strong> 34&ndash;38&nbsp;mm.</li>
      <li><strong>6.5&ndash;7&nbsp;in:</strong> 36&ndash;40&nbsp;mm.</li>
      <li><strong>7&ndash;7.5&nbsp;in:</strong> 38&ndash;42&nbsp;mm.</li>
      <li><strong>Over 7.5&nbsp;in:</strong> 40&ndash;46&nbsp;mm.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For bracelets, add about 0.5&nbsp;in (1.3&nbsp;cm) for a snug fit, 0.75&nbsp;in for comfort and 1&nbsp;in (2.5&nbsp;cm) for a loose drape. Thicker bracelets need a little more room than thin chains.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="change" aria-labelledby="change-h">
    <h2 id="change-h">Can You Change Your Wrist Size?</h2>
    <p>Adult wrist bones do not grow, so wrist circumference changes very little. Forearm work (wrist curls, farmer&rsquo;s carries, grip training) builds the forearm just above the wrist, and gaining or losing weight shifts the measurement by a few millimeters. Large wrists are often linked to a bigger frame, which can support more muscle; see where you stand with the <a href="/average-bicep-size/">average bicep size</a> and <a href="/average-shoulder-width/">average shoulder width</a> pages.</p>
  </section>

